<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation puts out the story that the first elevator was installed in 1880, and whenever they find an elevator created before that date, they either destroy it, conceal it, or make it look like a modern elevator.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;why? I think the crux of my problem is that this entire SCP relies on us assuming the Foundation has a good reason to conceal ancient elevators but there's not really like&#8230;any reason present here for us to assume that? Indeed, the existence of ancient elevators implies that people would naturally be pretty okay with them existing ages ago cos like&#8230;everyone would've seen them and be aware of it so it just feels weird that the Foundation intervene at all?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When it hits the surface, aliens pop out, and explain that 4000 yeas ago, they gave the gift of elevators to man, having misinterpreted instructions from the alien queen to &quot;uplift&quot; our species.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why did it take the aliens 4000 years to realise their mistake?</p>
<p>I don't really have big concerns other than these, but my concern is that not having good answers to these will mean this loses the verisimilitude this needs for us to accept the premise enough that the reveal doesn't land right.</p>
<p>I'm&#8230;also of a mind that leaving this open might be more effective than saying it was aliens: something like <a href="/scp-4466">SCP-4466</a> might be a good touchstone for this where it's like&#8230;something normal but treated in a very alien way and where the strangeness works cos there's no resolution to it? Like for me this would work best if there's a moment where the reader is like 'wait are these elevators' followed immediately by 'these are elevators but wrong', y'know? My concern with aliens is that it feels a little too much like a deus ex machina where the answer just, appears from thin air with no build up; and so it feels like the arc might feel unsatisfying? There's this big mystery about what these things are and instead of the foundation solving it, the answer just jumps out and says it's the answer, y'know?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

